.TH JOIN 1
.SH NAME
join \- relational join of two sorted files, in Limbo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B join
[
.BI -a\  n
|
.BI -v\  n
]
[
.BI -e\  string
]
[
.BI -o\  list
]
[
.BI -t\  char
]
[
.BI -1\  field
]
[
.BI -2\  field
]
.I file1 file2
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Join
is a Limbo-native workalike for a bounded subset of
.IR join (1),
compiled to Dis and run by
.IR emu ,
not a host binary \(em see
.B doc/plan9-port.md
in the source tree. It lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
.I Join
reads
.I file1
and
.IR file2 ,
each already sorted on its join field (this program does not sort them
itself, same as real
.IR join ),
and writes one output line for every pair of lines \(em one from each
file \(em whose join fields are equal: the join field's value, then
.IR file1 's
remaining fields, then
.IR file2 's
remaining fields. If either
.I file1
or
.I file2
is
.BR - ,
standard input is read for that one.
.PP
Lines with a repeated key on either side produce every combination, not
just the first: a key appearing twice in
.I file1
and twice in
.I file2
produces four output lines.
.PP
The default field separator is a run of one or more spaces and/or tabs
(leading whitespace before the first field is skipped entirely); the
default
.I output
field separator is always a single space, regardless of what the input
used.
.SS Options
.TP
.BI -a\  n
In addition to the default matched output, also emit a line for every
unpairable line in file
.I n
(1 or 2).
.TP
.BI -v\  n
Emit
.I only
the unpairable lines from file
.I n
\(em unlike
.BR -a ,
this suppresses the default matched output entirely.
.B -v 1
and
.B -v 2
may both be given.
.TP
.BI -e\  string
Replace an empty output field with
.I string
(most useful with
.B -a
and
.BR -o ,
where an unpaired line has nothing to contribute for the other file's
fields).
.TP
.BI -o\ list
Choose exactly which fields appear, and in what order, instead of the
default format.
.I List
is one or more comma-separated elements (repeatable
.B -o
options concatenate, in the order given), each either
.B 0
(the join field's value) or
.IB filenum . field
(1-based field
.I field
of file
.IR filenum ).
A field reference past the end of a line's actual fields \(em including
every
.IB n . field
reference for the file that has no matching line \(em reads as empty
(or the
.B -e
string).
.TP
.BI -t\  char
Use
.I char
as the field separator for splitting
.I and
for output, in place of the whitespace-run default; every occurrence of
.I char
is a separator (no collapsing).
.TP
.BI -1\  field
Join on
.IR field 's-th
field of
.I file1
(1-based) instead of the first.
.TP
.BI -2\  field
The same, for
.IR file2 .
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/join.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR jointest (1)
.SH BUGS
This is a deliberately bounded subset: no bare
.B -a
(historic, meaning both files at once), no
.BR -j / -j1 / -j2
(historic synonyms for
.BR -1 / -2 ),
no multi-argument
.B -o
(historic; use commas or repeat
.B -o
instead) \(em the real
.IR join (1)'s
own manual calls all of these "compatibility" options that "should not
be used", so none are ported.
.PP
Input is assumed already sorted on its join field, exactly like the
real oracle: this program does not sort, check, or warn, so unsorted
input can silently miss real matches once the merge scan has advanced
past them \(em checked against the host directly (an out-of-order key
in one file caused a real pair to go unreported, with no error), not
assumed to be safe.
.PP
A join-field index past the end of a particular line's fields compares
as the empty string rather than raising an error \(em also checked
against the host, which does the same (silently, exit status 0) rather
than treating it as malformed input.
.PP
Field-value comparison is plain byte-for-byte string comparison, not
locale-aware collation; for ordinary ASCII sorted input (the common
case for a "C" locale) this matches the host directly, but is not
guaranteed to match a locale-specific
.IR sort (1)
in every case.
.PP
Every rule above (default separator vs. output separator, cross-product
on duplicate keys, `-v` suppressing matched output entirely rather than
just adding to it, out-of-range field handling) was checked against the
host's own
.I join
(macOS/BSD, POSIX-conformant) with targeted probes before writing any of
this, not read off a manual from memory.
